# =============================================================================
# 1NKY — torrc for the .onion mirror
# =============================================================================
#
# What this is: one v3 onion service that forwards to caddy's plain-HTTP :8080
# vhost inside the compose network. The onion path is the sovereign path — it
# never crosses Cloudflare, and the box in front of it sees a Tor circuit, not
# a visitor. Nothing here needs an account, an email, or a certificate.
#
# NO-LOGS (CLAUDE.md hard rule #1). Two things keep that true:
#
#   * `Log notice stderr` below. At notice level tor emits its version, its
#     bootstrap progress, and warnings — never a client address, because a
#     hidden service does not learn one. Per-circuit and per-rendezvous detail
#     starts at `info`, so DO NOT raise this to `info` or `debug`, not even
#     "just to debug something". Lower it to `warn` if you want less.
#   * `SafeLogging 1` (also the default, stated for the avoidance of doubt)
#     scrubs sensitive strings out of anything tor does print.
#
#   Honest caveat for the auditor: tor's own *warnings* can name a Tor network
#     relay or directory authority by IP ("Problem bootstrapping ... host <fp>
#     at <addr>:<port>"). Those are public infrastructure addresses, never a
#     1NKY visitor. infra/README.md documents how the no-logs audit grep
#     accounts for it.
# =============================================================================

# --- Not a proxy, not a relay -----------------------------------------------
# This container makes no outbound requests on anyone's behalf, so there is no
# SOCKS listener to abuse and nothing on the network can borrow this Tor client.
SocksPort 0

# No ORPort / no DirPort / no ExitPolicy: publishing an onion service does not
# make this box part of the Tor network's routing, and we do not want it to be.

# --- State ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only the onion subdirectory is persisted (the tor_keys volume). Consensus and
# guard state under /var/lib/tor are deliberately ephemeral: they are cheap to
# re-fetch (~30s of bootstrap on restart) and nothing is gained by keeping them.
DataDirectory /var/lib/tor
RunAsDaemon 0

# --- The onion service ------------------------------------------------------
# The keys in this directory ARE the address. Lose them and the .onion changes
# forever and every published link dies, which is why it is a named volume
# (tor_keys) and not a bind mount into the repo tree.
HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/onion/
HiddenServiceVersion 3

# Virtual port 80 on the onion -> caddy's plain :8080 vhost. No TLS on that hop
# and none wanted: the .onion address IS the service's public key, so Tor has
# already authenticated and encrypted end to end. A certificate here would add
# a CA to a trust path that deliberately has none.
#
# GOTCHA: tor resolves this hostname ONCE, at startup. If caddy is *recreated*
# (not just restarted) it gets a new container IP and tor keeps talking to the
# old one. `docker compose restart tor` after any caddy recreate — the
# healthcheck in docker-compose.yml is what tells you it happened.
HiddenServicePort 80 caddy:8080

# Rate-limit introduction requests at the introduction points (tor >= 0.4.7),
# so a flood aimed at the onion is absorbed by the Tor network rather than
# arriving at a $6 droplet.
HiddenServiceEnableIntroDoSDefense 1

# --- Logging (read the header before changing this) -------------------------
Log notice stderr
SafeLogging 1
